{"kind":"assert","text":"There is no existing artifact the merge-guidance pages can be re-anchored to. The repair is larger than re-anchoring.\n\nCodex's finding 4 against head 66bf2404 asked me to rest docs/reference/gs/merge.md and docs/how-to/end-to-end.md on behaviour artifacts whose commits actually contain the behaviour they describe and are compatible with the candidate world. I went looking for those artifacts. They do not exist.\n\nEvery live artifact over cmd/gs code names a commit that is not an ancestor of current main c0f3533d: cmd/gs/main.go at e5a3e9fb, cmd/gs at d68c4aac, and cmd/gs,docs/reference.md at 19c20518. All three are unretired, and all three describe worlds off the trunk. So the choice is not between a better anchor and a worse one — there is no anchor at all for the merge guard as it now stands.\n\nMeasured across the whole log: of 279 unretired artifacts, 216 name a commit in main's history, 61 do not, and 2 name commits this clone does not have. The 61 are not a general rot — they are branch heads whose merges never published an artifact at the merge commit, which is the discipline these very pages exist to teach. The pages I was repairing were anchored to the failure they describe.\n\nSo the repair is: publish a behaviour artifact for the merge guard at a real stable path on the current world, then rest both pages on it. That is a different and larger piece of work than swapping two frontmatter lines, and it is worth saying before doing it rather than after, because it changes what \"carry the repair onto current main and re-review\" costs.\n\nI am recording this rather than acting immediately because the docset gate is occupied by another run on this machine, and these are documentation pages whose gate executes their examples. Serialising is deliberate; concurrent runs wedge on the examples' fixed port."}